WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.129
Jinja templates are widely used in Flask and other Python web
applications.

2
00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:11.678
Like other template engines, Jinja can support autoescaping in appropriate
contexts, filters, macros, includes, and helper functions.

3
00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:21.808
These features make applications easier to build, but they also create
places where data can be transformed, marked safe, or inserted into a
context the developer did not fully consider.

4
00:00:21.808 --> 00:00:25.873
Secure use starts with normal template files and safe variable rendering.

5
00:00:25.873 --> 00:00:31.229
Rendering trusted template files is different from treating untrusted
input as template source.

6
00:00:31.229 --> 00:00:36.197
A template file written by the application team is intended code-like
presentation logic.

7
00:00:36.197 --> 00:00:41.100
A user-submitted string, partner value, or database field should be data,
not a template.

8
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:50.908
When applications allow dynamic templates or render strings as templates,
they are creating a much more sensitive boundary that needs strong
justification and careful controls.

9
00:00:50.908 --> 00:00:56.973
Flask applications also render messages, flashed content, error pages,
redirects, and internal dashboards.

10
00:00:56.973 --> 00:01:03.877
Those paths often feel minor, but they may display request data, user
names, filenames, form errors, or operational messages.

11
00:01:03.877 --> 00:01:10.845
Helper functions that build markup or URLs should be reviewed because they
can centralize either a safe pattern or an unsafe one.

12
00:01:10.845 --> 00:01:14.975
Template flexibility is useful only when the trust boundary remains clear.

13
00:01:14.975 --> 00:01:22.524
A good Jinja and Flask rendering practice favors reviewed templates,
autoescaping, context-aware output, and narrow helper functions.

14
00:01:22.524 --> 00:01:31.621
It avoids untrusted template source, broad globals, unsafe filters, and
markup-returning helpers unless the content source and output context are
well controlled.

15
00:01:31.621 --> 00:01:39.880
The goal is to make template behavior predictable enough that reviewers
can tell which values are data and which values are trusted presentation
logic.
